<<~ kanawai "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119">>



<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Pronaos ~ the house's first face, without a landlord

> Status: PROPOSED — design carrier, not an implementation receipt. The operator chose the composed
> Pronaos as the production target on 2026-09-21. Node-owned and separate-static arrangements remain
> contract witnesses, never competing vessel ontologies; no composed carrier deployment receipt stands here.

Greek *pronaos* names the front hall or fore-porch immediately before a temple's inner house. Pausanias names
Athena and Hermes among the *Pronaoi* standing before the Ismenian Apollo sanctuary at Thebes; that narrow
evidence supports a local threshold figure, never a universal Hermes-Pronaos cult. The Pronaos carries the
relation into DreamNet: a particular Lararium's truthful first face. It gives a traveler public
orientation and declared crossings while the local house retains its own identity, archive, judgment, and keys.

Sources: LSJ, https://atlas.perseus.tufts.edu/dictionaries/entry/urn%3Acite2%3Ascaife-viewer%3Adictionaries.v1%3Alsj-n88071/
and Pausanias 9.10.2, https://www.theoi.com/Text/Pausanias9A.html. The architectural rhyme of public sight
without cella access remains a studied possibility, not a runtime claim.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/mythic-binding>>

!! The mythic binding ~ a forecourt for one house, not another road tier

Greek ''pronaos'' names the front hall or fore-porch immediately before a temple's inner house. The narrow
Theban evidence places Athena and Hermes among the *Pronaoi* before the Ismenian Apollo sanctuary; it supports
the image of a local threshold presence, never a universal Hermes-Pronaos cult. The architectural term carries
the useful relation: a traveler stands before this house, receives its declared bearings, and still has not
entered its inner authority.

The Elyncian Lares Viales already hold the road and the Herm already marks the turning. The Pronaos therefore
names neither carriage nor crossroads. It names the ''intimate first face'' of one composition:

```text
Compitum       roads meet
Herm           carriage and public crossing stand
Pronaos        this house presents its declared first face
Lararium       persona, documents, keys, and held local material remain within
```

Hermes in this binding names escort and threshold relation, not a gatekeeper who owns the visitor or the house.
The Pronaos may present a public route toward a peer; it cannot turn that presentation into identity, admission,
read authority, or document ownership. The English ''veil'' remains the read-scope ladder; the Pronaos remains the
place where a local composition makes a truthful outward face.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-placement>>

!! The placement ~ the forecourt attached to a particular house

```
worldly name / LAN name / link / QR
  → Compitum / crossroads: roads meet
  → Herm floor: carriage, public floor, peer crossing
  → Pronaos: this local composition's first face and declared public paths
  → Lararium inner house: persona, owned documents, held local material
```

The Herm remains the floor every vessel may stand on. The Lararium remains the optional lift that seats a
persona and its document sovereignty. The Pronaos forms neither a third vessel type nor a new peer identity. It
names the first face a local composition may light from held public capabilities. A Node process, static artifact
shore, reverse proxy, container composition, or future carrier may realize it; no physical placement changes the
capability relation.

''Only a lararium lights a Pronaos'' (operator-ruled). The temple rung belongs to the house: the arrival page, the
arrival descriptor, the first-arrival kindle, and the house's face. A herm stands on the lower rungs of the ladder
below (#/the-rung-ladder): it hands over the bulb by name and, at most, posts a waymark. A herm handed any Pronaos
input refuses before a face listens.

''A herm MIRRORS BY CID'' (operator-ruled). A herm may carry a house's PUBLISHED Pronaos bytes by CID, as
carriage — for availability, never trust: the arrival page, a seed, an offered edition, one artifact an operator's
publication record names. A mirror presents no face, lights no Pronaos, and signs no public claim. Trust stays
with the house: a mirror the descriptor lists never kindles, and first arrival trusts the house's own origin
(`packages/lararium-node/src/kindle.ts`). So one law reads in three places: the FACE belongs to the lararium; the
publication record governs CARRIAGE of one named artifact; and the mirror carries bytes a house already published.

The public Librarium — a public crossroads shrine of books, images, media, and deliberately offered editions —
waits outside this carrier. A Pronaos may later route an explicit Librarium projection. It never turns the
house-held `library:` collection or arbitrary CAS bytes public.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-rung-ladder>>

!! The rung ladder ~ household, crossroads, temple

How much a vessel says about itself to a stranger climbs three rungs, the same three tiers Elyncia keeps:
a household shrine, a crossroads marker, a temple. ''Each rung opts in above the one below; the default stays
silent.'' Grounds: <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/shelter-against-empire#/legibility">> — a
self-description reads as the two-finger sign, cheap to read at a distance — and
<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/siege-resilience#/the-active-prober">> — any surface that answers a
stranger distinguishably reads as an oracle, so the client proves first.

```
SILENT    herm (default)   hands over the bulb BY NAME: /bulb/<cid>.bin, /cas/<cid>; describes nothing
WAYMARK   herm (opt-in)    + one UNSIGNED descriptor at /.well-known/lar: format, route shapes, bulb CID
TEMPLE    lararium only    its own Pronaos: the arrival page and the arrival descriptor (#/the-first-arrival)
ORACLE    every vessel     NO rung: the FLOW-map crosses only the gated oracle socket, to a dialer that proved first
```

''SILENT.'' The traveler brings the name. The bulb names itself by its genesis seed's CID, which re-derives from
the published build, so a traveler arrives already knowing it, and most Nexuses start from the stock bulb.
''One seed, one CID'': the CID hashes the `seed.json` bytes as the genesis build publishes them, the herm hands over
those exact bytes at `/bulb/<cid>.bin`, and a temple names the same bytes `seedCid` at `/genesis/seed.json`, so a
traveler holding the stock seed CID finds the same coal at either door. No route lists, indexes or describes what
the herm holds; a listing answers only a CID the client names. Every unknown, withheld or refused request — a
path no face claims, a CID the herm never held, a wrong method, the well-known name itself — draws ''one
indistinguishable closed door'': the same status, headers and body, carrying no text that names what answered.

''WAYMARK.'' A herm that chooses discovery sets `herm.waymark` in its node config
(`~/.lares/config.json`) and serves one minimal descriptor: its format, its route shapes and its bulb CID. It names
no kindle pointer, no mirror and no carried Nexus. ''A herm never signs anything public'' — a public signature
under a carrier's key would correlate the place with every Nexus it carries. The waymark and the temple's
descriptor hold one dispatcher key, so one vessel can never answer the well-known name twice.

''TEMPLE.'' The lararium's own Pronaos keeps its arrival descriptor whole (#/the-first-arrival). A lararium handed
the waymark knob refuses: its arrival rides its Pronaos. Its readiness answer, `/api/health`, binds on a lararium
alone; on a herm that path draws the closed door, since a readiness answer describes the self.

''THE ORACLE STANDS ON NO RUNG.'' A vessel's oracle face shows no HTTP face at any rung: every HTTP path under it,
the pointer and the snapshot included, draws the one closed door. The signed FLOW-map pointer and the snapshot it
names cross only the vessel's oracle socket, behind the vessel's own gate handshake: the dialer proves its key
first, the gate signs its verdict with the vessel key, and only then do the pointer frame and the snapshot frame
leave. A stranger who opens the socket and proves nothing receives the gate's challenge and its refusal, and no
frame of the map. The carriage, a browser vessel's reader and the mesh witnesses all read through that socket.

The vessel key signs the pointer, the same key that signs the vessel's carry seals, and a ''proven peer''
names anyone who mints a key and proves it: the gate checks possession, not standing. So the pointer hands every
such dialer a signature that ties this place to every Nexus whose carry seals the vessel key signs, the very
correlation the waymark paragraph guards against. The socket narrows who reads it to dialers who prove first; it
does not unbind the correlation. The per-Nexus wire key owes that cut at the re-found, where each Nexus's wire
signs under its own key and no one key spans two. Before any proof the gate's `lar:challenge` carries a fresh nonce
alone and echoes no gate key (the dialer holds the pin) (`auth-wire.ts`, `LarChallengeMsg`); it still tells an opener that a gate answers — owed to
the proof-first protocol lane, where the dialer proves first and a stranger draws silence.

''THE BULB CARRIES THE COAL ALONE'' (operator-ruled; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/vessel-caps#/the-five-caps">>).
The bulb carries the genesis seed and the CAS blobs that seed names, and nothing that names a house or a Nexus —
so two houses holding one genesis hold one bulb CID. The corm holds the charter epoch as its freshness lease; a house's
joining pointers ride the stolon, the invite. Neither rides the bulb.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-five-relations>>

!! Five relations ~ reach, browser trust, identity, delivery, authority

```
name          grants a rendezvous hint
secure origin grants browser-secure-context eligibility
peer key      proves peer identity
route         selects a declared delivery target
capability    permits a particular action
```

No relation grants the next. DNS and certificate custody can withhold or alter an outward signpost; they cannot
claim a peer key, read a private bag, resolve a merge, or admit a member. A peer key survives a name move. Cache
presence carries a previously acquired response, never authority, membership, freshness truth, or causal order.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-cap-stack>>

!! The Pronaos cap stack ~ the forecourt holds public delivery, never inner reach

```
#has {origin-attestation}       — local DNS/domain/LAN reach and browser TLS boundary
#has {arrival-projection}       — exact public Web and worker artifact inventory
#has {bootstrap-delivery}       — `seed.json` and seed-named, CID-checked CAS members
#has {public-route-map}         — declared paths, methods, targets, and refusal shapes
#has {delivery-policy}          — CSP, SRI where browser subresources use it, scope, cache, headers
#has {transport-introduction}   — a bounded public live path, if declared

#has-NOT {peer-identity}
#has-NOT {persona}
#has-NOT {document-write}
#has-NOT {general-cas-read}
#has-NOT {library-traversal}
#has-NOT {VEIL-read}
#has-NOT {oracle-authority}
#has-NOT {merge-authority}
#has-NOT {admission-authority}
```

The existing `VEIL ⊂ PERSONAGROUP ⊂ CONTRACT ⊂ PUBLIC` ladder controls read scope. The Pronaos controls this
house's first-reach presentation and delivery. Their rhyme grants no license to fuse them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-declared-path>>

!! The declared path ~ a route never grants ambient reach

Every outward path carries a positive declaration: a finite prepared artifact inventory, a seed-derived CAS
member, one explicit public projection, or one bounded live introduction. No route says "serve a directory," "read
any CID," or "fall through to the application." Public cache copies may hold only already-declared public bytes.

```
GET /                        → selected index bytes
GET /assets/<declared>       → selected worker or Web artifact bytes
GET /genesis/seed.json       → selected seed bytes
GET /genesis/cas/<cid>       → seed-named CID-checked bytes
GET /public/<declared>       → separately declared public projection
GET /live/<declared> upgrade → bounded transport introduction
```

SRI verifies direct browser subresources where the browser consumes it. Seed and CAS members rest on the
project's own CID/hash verification. A WebSocket `Origin` check protects a browser-facing introduction; it never
replaces the later peer-key handshake or the served vessel's capability decision.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-procession>>

!! The forecourt ~ first reach unfolds in four local acts

# ''Reach.'' A human or leaf vessel follows a local name, contracted domain, LAN answer, QR, or remembered hint.
# ''Stand before.'' The Pronaos returns only declared public arrival material and public route information.
# ''Verify.'' The leaf checks CIDs and expected peer keys; domain and certificate remain reach witnesses, never peer proof.
# ''Cross.'' The leaf dials a declared transport. The served vessel separately proves the peer relation and permits or refuses the requested action.

This sequence keeps the user's leaf vessel sovereign. It does not ask the person to log into a server; it gives the
local vessel enough truthful material to choose its next crossing.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-first-arrival>>

!! The first arrival ~ the house's own origin serves the kindle (RULED, BUILT)

''The first arrival trusts the lararium's own origin'' (operator-ruled). A device that has never booted a
vessel holds nothing to check bytes against; whatever origin it first reaches decides what it runs. So that
origin MUST stand as the house itself: ''the lararium's own Pronaos serves the kindle'' — the arrival page, the
worker, `genesis/seed.json`, and the seed-named CAS members. Household operators reach it through
DNS→home-LAN integrations, so the name a household types resolves to its own house on its own network.

''Hash-pinned static copies stand as MIRRORS, never as the arrival.'' Each mirror rests on ''its own origin
per CID'', and a mirror never shares an origin with the house or with another CID's mirror. Three facts of
the browser close the other reading:

* ''A phone cannot hash-check a top-level page.'' The browser runs whatever bytes the navigated origin
  returns; no user-held pin gates the document itself.
* ''SRI covers subresources only'' — scripts and stylesheets a page already trusts — never the page that
  declares the integrity attributes.
* ''Signed Exchanges (SXG)'', the one standard that let a page's bytes prove themselves apart from their
  origin, ended (2026-09-30). No browser path remains to make a static copy speak with the house's authority.

So a mirror names a CID-checked copy a vessel already kindled may fetch and verify; it never kindles one.

''The arrival descriptor stands at a well-known URI'' (RFC 8615, `/.well-known/…`) on the house's origin, so
any client finds it without a route map. ''Content negotiation stays an extra door'' onto the same
descriptor, never the only one, and never a second source of truth.

''The arrival page answers at the floor.'' A lararium standing at its waking floor still serves its arrival
page: liveness ⊥ readiness. A house that cannot yet open its archive stays reachable and says what it cannot
do (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-arrival-page">>).

''Built.'' The descriptor answers at `/.well-known/lar` — `lar` names the namespace, so the one name covers every
vessel — as `application/vnd.lar.arrival+json`, canonical JSON derived from the route receipt alone: the arrival
page, the seed and its CID, each worker asset and seed-named CAS member, and the mirrors by CID. A request for `/`
naming that media type reaches the identical bytes; a wildcard or an HTML navigation still reads the page
(`lararium-node/src/pronaos-adapter.ts`). A mirror list (`LAR_PRONAOS_MIRRORS`) refuses before mount when a mirror
names a CID the receipt does not, stands on any origin the house answers on, or shares its origin with another
entry (`pronaos-composition.ts`). `main.ts` feeds it every origin the house answers on through `houseOriginsOf`:
each reach face's origin on every standing, plus each composed Web, relay and oracle origin. The kindle reads the
descriptor, the seed and every seed-named member off the house's own origin, verifies each against its CID, never
dials a mirror, and mints the device's own key (`kindle.ts#pullArrival`). `pullArrival` stands as a LIBRARY call,
exported beside `pullBulb`; no CLI verb carries it. The composition reads no standing and no archive and runs
before the boot reads either, so a house at its waking floor serves all of it.

''The descriptor never carries the floor's announcement.'' That announcement speaks to PEERS over the socket or
relay, once per carried Nexus, and never at `/.well-known/lar`
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-announcement">>). So the bytes a floor serves here
equal the bytes a raised hearth serves.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/refusal-and-loss>>

!! Refusal and loss ~ named outward failure, detailed local witness

Undeclared, private, traversal, and missing requests receive one uniform outward refusal shape so the boundary
does not become an existence oracle. A known public integrity mismatch names an integrity failure. A known public
live target that cannot answer names temporary unavailability. Browser and operator diagnostics may carry the
specific local cause; external responses do not reveal whether a private object exists.

Integrity-addressable bytes may still carry `no-store`. Each route declares `immutable`, `revalidate`, or `no-store`
as its delivery class; a CID or hash never infers an HTTP cache policy. Selectors, discovery, and mutable public
declarations carry explicit revalidation or `no-store` policy. No `Date.now()`, cache timestamp, certificate date, or
other wall-clock witness may decide shared ordering, membership, integrity, admission, or authority.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-production-shape>>

!! The production shape ~ one house's forecourt, while every shore keeps its proof

The production direction composes a static Web arrival shore and a live Node shore as one operator-held house's
Pronaos. Each shore retains its own capability stack and exact response witness. Node-owned serving and a
separate static shore remain test arrangements that must produce the same route, integrity, refusal, and
peer-symmetry observations. They prevent the composition root from becoming an untested metaphysical claim.

The route and delivery receipt belongs to the operator-held deployment configuration. A smaller public arrival
declaration may announce safe public facts. It carries no private routes, DNS credentials, broad artifact access,
or peer-identity substitution. Origin-attestation custody and delegated-DNS governance remain a separate crossed
design wave; they may configure reach and never create document or peer authority.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-red-witness>>

!! The red witness ~ prove the approach before machinery grows

The first witness attributes `/`, one worker, `genesis/seed.json`, and one seed-named CAS member to their named
serving process, compares each response with expected bytes, and asserts the declared cache class independently of
the integrity receipt. The current byte shore keeps `/` and `genesis/seed.json` `no-store`; selected assets and CAS
members may receive `immutable` only through explicit route policy. It then weakens one boundary at a time:

* widen the route inventory, traverse a path, or request an undeclared CID;
* return HTML for a worker, seed, CAS, or missing route;
* substitute wrong bytes under a valid-looking CID;
* broaden worker scope without an explicit declaration;
* derive `ws` from an HTTPS arrival or use a stale configured self-URL;
* accept a disallowed browser Origin;
* infer peer identity from DNS, TLS, or host text;
* infer an HTTP cache class from a CID or hash;
* let a cache hit serve an undeclared/private route;
* turn downstream loss into a successful SPA fallback;
* use a wall-clock value as authority or shared order.

Every weakening MUST read red. The positive path proves secure-context boot, explicit worker scope, a bounded live
introduction, separate peer-key proof, and separate capability/admission refusal.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/operator-stories>>

!! Operator stories ~ Gaia and Elyncia

**Operators on Gaia, Earth circa 2026, SHALL need** a household, library, cooperative, school, clinic, or civic
AI practice to offer a secure local arrival surface through a LAN name or contracted domain while documents,
identity keys, and causal state remain local and portable.

**Operators on Gaia SHALL need** browser boot through HTTPS and WAN loss without device-installed CAs,
account-centric hosting, or silent hosted authority.

**Operators on Elyncia, the Orichalcum Mythpunk RPG, SHALL need** an approach where a traveler recognizes a
shrine, receives declared bearings, verifies a crossing, and retains sovereignty over their own leaf vessel.

**Operators on Elyncia SHALL need** independent shrines and Nexuses to raise approaches toward one another
without making any approach keeper of every archive, persona, or causal island it reaches.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/next-cuts>>

!! Next cuts ~ one contract before deployment variants

# Write pure route-inventory and uniform-refusal controls.
# Re-home the present boot-byte adapter under arrival/bootstrap projection language; retain its current code name only until tests cover the new contract.
# Add process attribution and exact byte/CID controls for index, worker, seed, and seed-named CAS members.
# Add secure-context, request-derived scheme/self-URL, worker-scope, CSP, and SRI controls.
# Add one same-origin bounded live introduction with Origin refusal, peer-key proof, and separate capability/admission decision.
# Run the common witness through Node-owned, separate-static, and composed-Pronaos arrangements.
# Exercise a persona-less Herm floor and persona-bearing Lararium lift against the same public arrival projection while proving separate authority.
# Exercise a domain move, split-horizon LAN reach, and cached-public-byte continuity; leave DNS/ACME implementation on its crossed governance lane.
# After every major cut, run Aftermath, Evergreen, L-Prime, and reality-alpha cleanup before the next integration wave.

<<~/ahu>>

